13 lawyers representing  37 petitioners are asking supreme court  justices  to scrap an anti-terror law the  legislation's  championed by president rodrigo duterte  and backed  by the military but the scale of this  legal challenge to the government  is unprecedented any one of us can  actually be branded as a terrorist  based on the definition of terrorism in  the law for example as i speak now  they can interpret this as terrorism  which is also our message to the  filipino people  we should get involved we should be  informed about this we should study  what this law is all about because this  is not just about us  lawyers we are fighting for our civil  liberties and for our future  the anti-terror law broadens the  definition of terrorism  and allows the military to expand  surveillance and detain  suspects for weeks without charge  the government says it needs the law to  fight insurgent groups  in many parts of the country and the  national security adviser says  it is designed to protect civil  liberties  it's a democratic process  we should allow this to happen terrorism  is real  critics say the government has  weaponized the law to systematically  stifle dissent  democracy they say is under siege this  is why they turn to the supreme court  they say  as the filipino people's last line of  defense against tyranny  jamella alindogan al jazeera manila
 

